// tutuca script: the language a view file is written in.
//
// It has two halves. The SLOT language — what an attribute value holds — is
// the subset that is a single expression. The BLOCK language, `<script
// type="tutuca/script">`, adds declarations, statements and operators over the
// same atoms; §2.4 of the design is the promise that it invents no new way to
// NAME a value, which is why an author who can read a view can read a handler.
//
// Each half currently has its OWN tokenizer and parser (`lex.mbt`/`parse.mbt`
// for slots, `script_lex.mbt`/`script_parse.mbt` for blocks). That is a
// staging decision rather than a design one, and `script_lex.mbt`'s header
// states the condition for undoing it: the two become one once the block
// grammar has proven itself. Until then this comment describes two, because
// claiming one and shipping two is the kind of thing a reader trusts once.
//
// It lives above `core` rather than inside it because `core` never parses
// anything: the only production callers of the slot parser are `anode` (every
// directive and `<x>` op) and `component` (`provide` / `lookup`). What `core`
// cannot give up is the parsed form — `Step::ScopeBindStep` embeds a `Expr` so
// a rebuilt render stack can replay its bindings, and that cycle is why the
// value and path layers share one package to begin with. So the AST stays in
// `core` and everything that produces or reports on one lives here.
//
// Pure and target-agnostic like `statedef` and `viewgen`: text in, typed
// values and diagnostics out, no filesystem. It ships to the browser twice
// over — the playground's generator and the card runtime both carry it — so
// it takes no dependency `core` does not already have.
import {
  "moonbitlang/core/debug",
  "marianoguerra/tutuca/core" @tutuca,
  "moonbitlang/core/string",
}
